<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juqu Wuhui</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 444</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juqu Wuhui was a prince of the Northern Liang dynasty in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juqu-mengxun">Juqu Mengxun</a>. After Northern Wei conquered the Northern Liang in 439, he and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juqu-anzhou">Juqu Anzhou</a> led the remnants of the family west, took Shanshan and in 442 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaochang">Gaochang</a> (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turpan</a>), where Wuhui ruled as king until his death in 444.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The date of Juqu Wuhui's birth is not known. He first appears in 437, as governor of Jiuquan Commandery under his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juqu-mujian">Juqu Mujian</a>. After Northern Wei took the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wuwei">Guzang</a> and captured Mujian in 439, Wuhui, joined by his brother Juqu Yide, abandoned Jiuquan and fled first to Jinchang and then to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a>. In the spring of 440 he struck back, captured the Northern Wei general Yuan Jie outside Jiuquan and retook the city, but he failed to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zhangye">Zhangye</a>.</p><p>In the autumn of 440 he offered his submission through his general Liang Wei, returning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiuquan">Jiuquan</a> and Yuan Jie, and in the spring of 441 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-taiwu-of-northern-wei">Emperor Taiwu of Northern Wei</a> made him Prince of Jiuquan. That summer his cousin Juqu Tang'er rebelled at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> and was killed in battle, but Northern Wei, still suspicious, sent its general Daxi Juan to besiege Jiuquan, which fell by the winter of 441.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Short of food at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunhuang">Dunhuang</a> and fearing a new attack, Wuhui resolved to rebuild his state in the west. In 442 he abandoned Dunhuang and joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juqu-anzhou">Juqu Anzhou</a> before Shanshan; half his soldiers died of thirst on the way, but the king of Shanshan fled and Wuhui took the city.</p><p>At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaochang">Gaochang</a> the former Northern Liang general Hei Shuang had asked for his help against Tang Qi, but then refused him entry. In the autumn of 442 Wuhui took Gaochang by surprise, and Hei fled to Rouran. Wuhui moved his headquarters there and sent envoys to the Liu Song capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jiankang">Jiankang</a>, submitting as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> and seeking an alliance, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emperor-wen-of-liu-song">Emperor Wen of Liu Song</a> created him Prince of Hexi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Wuhui died in 444, and his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juqu-anzhou">Juqu Anzhou</a> succeeded him, continuing to use the title of Prince of Hexi that their father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juqu-mengxun">Juqu Mengxun</a> had used before them. Wuhui's son was Juqu Ganshou. Chinese historians dispute whether Wuhui and Anzhou should be counted among the rulers of the Northern Liang, and most consider <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juqu-mujian">Juqu Mujian</a> its final prince.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Juqu Wuhui” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His brothers <b>Juqu Wuhui</b> and Juqu Anzhou fled west and ruled Gaochang from 442 until the Rouran Khaganate killed Anzhou in 460.</blockquote><a href="/state/northern-liang">Northern Liang</a></li></ul></section>
